Every new website starts ready to use:

- six published pages: a home page called "Welcome to" and the website's name, About, Contact (with a contact form
  whose messages go to the first admin), a privacy notice, terms of use and an accessibility statement;
- a main menu with About and Contact, and a footer menu with the other three pages;
- a logo, an icon and a picture for sharing on social media, made from the website's name;
- the roles admin, editor, contributor and member (see [Roles and permissions](../reference/roles-and-permissions.md)).

The ready-made pages are in English, whatever the website's language. Change or translate them in the admin panel.

If you leave out something a command needs, it asks for it, one question at a time, and also asks the optional
questions that have a usual answer. Every command asks the same way:

- a question that ends with "Press Enter for" and a value takes that value when you just press Enter;
- a question that lists choices accepts one of them, in capitals or not;
- a password is not shown as you type it, and is never printed; you type it twice, and if the two are not the same
  you are asked for it again;
- some answers are read back in words for you to confirm, for example a language code;
- a wrong answer is explained and asked again; after 3 wrong answers in a row the command stops and changes
  nothing;
- at the end, the command lists your answers and asks "Is all of this right?". Answer `no` to go through the
  questions again, with your answers kept: press Enter for each one that was right.

If you give everything on the command line, nothing is asked. Add `--no-interaction` (or `-n`) to never be asked:
then a missing or wrong value stops the command with a sentence that says what is wrong.

`<slug>` below is the short name of a website, the one you gave `site:create`, such as `demo`.

Search engines and AI readers are only ever offered pages that are:

- **published** (drafts and pages waiting for review never are);
- **public**;
- **not marked** "Ask search engines and AI search engines not to list this page";
- **not a copy** of a page on another website (a page with an "Address of the original").

While SightPress runs anywhere other than production (the `APP_ENV` setting, for example on your own computer), every
page asks search engines not to list it, and `robots.txt` keeps crawlers out of the whole website.
